# No unilateral behavior change

Keep in sync with `~/.cursor/rules/no-unilateral-behavior-change.mdc` when that file changes.

NEVER invent, reinterpret, replace, or “fix away” **product behavior on your own authority** when the human did not ask for that change.

## When this blocks (AI-initiated)

Stop, propose options, describe trade-offs, ask, and wait — only when **you** would:

- Change behavior the human already described to something else they did not request
- Pick a product-rule side they have not chosen (triggers, rising-edge vs value-must-change, re-fire, etc.)
- Add or redefine product semantics they never asked for

## When this does NOT block (human-directed)

No extra “approve the behavior” step when the human already directed the change in this conversation. Go ahead and edit code, specs, OpenSpec proposal/design/tasks, or skills that implement **what they asked for**, including:

- `/opsx-propose`, “write the proposal”, “add X”, “fix Y”, “do this in the current change”
- OpenSpec deltas that encode their stated requirements
- Clarifications they already answered (A/B choices, named semantics)

Asking again for approval of their own request is wrong.

## Includes (when AI would invent/replace)

- Rising-edge vs value-must-change vs re-fire-on-same-report style rules
- Trigger, condition, and automation semantics the human stated — do not swap them without asking
- Any conflict between your diagnosis and a human-stated rule — surface the conflict; do not pick a side by implementing

## Also does not block

- Restoring behavior that already matches what the human described
- Pure bugs with no product-rule ambiguity (crash, wrong pointer) when the intended behavior is already explicit
- Formatting, renames with no semantic change
